.TH HSH 1 "January 2026" "Holberton School" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command line interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
.RI [ command ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command line interpreter written in C.

It displays a prompt and waits for the user to type a command.
The command line is executed and the prompt is displayed again.

The shell works in both interactive and non-interactive modes.

.SH INVOCATION
.B Interactive mode
.br
When hsh is started without arguments, it runs in interactive mode
and displays a prompt.

.EX
$ ./hsh
($) ls
file1 file2
($) exit
.EE

.B Non-interactive mode
.br
Commands can be piped into hsh.

.EX
$ echo "ls" | ./hsh
file1 file2
.EE

.SH BUILTINS
The following built-in commands are implemented:

.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell.
If no argument is provided, the shell exits with the status of the
last executed command.

.TP
.B env
Print the current environment variables, one per line.

.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
hsh executes commands found in the directories listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable.

If a command is not found, an error message is printed to standard error.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
The shell uses the following environment variables:

.TP
.B PATH
Specifies the directories to search for executable commands.

.TP
.B SHLVL
Shell level.

.SH RETURN VALUE
hsh returns the exit status of the last command executed.
If a command is not found, the return status is 127.

.SH ERRORS
When a command cannot be found, hsh prints an error message in the
following format:

.EX
./hsh: <line_number>: <command>: not found
.EE

.SH LIMITATIONS
This shell does not support:
.IP
- Command separators (;)
.IP
- Pipes (|)
.IP
- Redirections (<, >, >>)
.IP
- Wildcards (*)
.IP
- Quotes or escape characters

